"use strict";
module.exports = /*#__PURE__*/JSON.parse('{"generatedAt":"2025-08-26T20:50:19.025Z","total":2,"tags":["memory","ml","post","project"],"items":[{"id":"L1VzZXJzL2tldmlud2FuZy9PYnNpZGlhbi9Ub3dhcmRzIExvbmctQ29udGV4dCBSZWFzb25pbmcubWQ=","slug":"towards-long-context-reasoning","section":"blog","sections":["ml"],"title":"Towards Long-Context Reasoning","date":"2025-08-26T20:50:18.704Z","tags":["ml","post","project"],"displayUrl":null,"image":"/Images/long-context-post.png?v=1756216544025","description":null,"links":[],"markdown":"---\\n# Introduction\\n\\nModern-day Large Language Models (LLMs) are all variants of the transformer architecture, which alternates layers of sequence mixers (attention blocks) and feature mixers (MLPs). Attention blocks move information across tokens in a sequence by calculating a similarity score between a given token, the query token, and each previous token, the key tokens, and using this similarity score to scale the amount each key\'s learned value vector is added to the query token\'s residual stream, whereas feature mixers only transform tokens within its hidden dimension. Because of this, when the sequence length $n$ grows large, attention layers become a massive computational bottleneck. Long-context computation is essential for tasks like code reasoning and generation, and for problem-solving tasks where chain-of-thought models thrive.\\n\\nThis is one reason why LLMs have a maximum context length, typically ~250k for frontier models. The other is that many models will use Rotary Positional Embeddings (RoPE) to encode the position of a token, which applies a rotation to the complex plane for query and key vectors. However, because such rotations are inherently sinusoidal (i.e. they repeat with a period), so past a certain context length, the transformations applied by RoPE will begin to wrap around. Approaches like YaRN gradually extend RoPE to infinite-length context lengths during pre-training by rescaling the position indices before applying RoPE, but this still doesn\'t fully solve the time complexity issue.\\n\\nBecause language models are autoregressive, we can decrease the time complexity for next-token generation from $O(n^2)$ to $O(n)$ by storing our newly generated keys and values at every turn, so we only need to compute similarity scores for the token we are currently generating, instead of all previous tokens. However, we still run into issues: for large $n$, we won\'t have enough storage on VRAM to cache increasing numbers of high-dimensional key and value vectors. Moreover, to generate those $n$ tokens, our total time complexity would be $O(n^2)$, which is still too expensive for million-length contexts. So, KV cache compression isn\'t enough.\\n\\n### Linear Attention\\n\\nIn comes linear attention: instead of caching every individual key and value, what if we store a fixed-size matrix $M$ that the model has learned to compress all of the important past context? Concretely, if our keys are $k_1, \\\\dots, k_{n-1}$ and our values are $v_1, \\\\dots, v_{n-1},$ when generating token $n$, we can compute our query $q_n$ and then multiply by $M_n$ to derive the output $o_n = M_n q_n \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}^d$. This gives us a simple calculation that is not more computationally intensive as $n$ increases. But, how do we update $M$?\\n\\nIn vanilla attention for iterative inference, we use \\n$$\\no_n = \\\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\\n\\n\\\\frac{\\\\exp\\\\!\\\\left( \\\\frac{q_n \\\\cdot k_i}{\\\\sqrt{d}} \\\\right)}\\n\\n{\\\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1} \\\\exp\\\\!\\\\left( \\\\frac{q_n \\\\cdot k_j}{\\\\sqrt{d}} \\\\right)}\\n\\nv_i\\n$$\\nto generate the outputs for the next token. If we want to turn this into the form of $M_n q_n$ for some updating matrix $M_n,$ we want some function $\\\\phi$ such that $\\\\kappa(q,k) \\\\triangleq \\\\exp(\\\\tfrac{qk}{\\\\sqrt{d}}) = \\\\phi(q)^T \\\\phi(k).$ Equipped with this factorization, we are now able to express the attention calculation as\\n$$\\no_n = \\\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\\n\\n\\\\frac{\\\\phi(q_n)^T\\\\phi(k_i)}{\\\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1} \\\\phi(q_n)^T\\\\phi(k_j)} v_i^T\\n\\n= \\\\frac{\\\\sum_{i=1} ^{n} \\\\phi(q_n)^T\\\\phi(k_i)v_i^T}{\\\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1} \\\\phi(q_n)^T\\\\phi(k_j)} \\n= \\\\frac{\\\\phi(q_n)^T \\\\left(\\\\sum_{i=1} ^{n-1} \\\\phi(k_i)v_i^T\\\\right)}{\\\\phi(q_n)^T\\\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}\\\\phi(k_j)}\\n$$\\nwhich means that now, we can use a \\"prefix sum\\" to make $O(1)$ updates to the state matrix $M$. A simple example of such a $\\\\phi$ is $\\\\max(0,Wx)$.\\n\\nHowever, errors will accumulate with only finite memory. For simplicity, we ignore $\\\\phi$, or alternatively, assume it is the identity; we also ignore the (normalizing) denominator. Then, $o_n = q_n^T \\\\sum _{i=1} ^{n-1} k_iv_i^T = M_nq_n$ where $M_n = \\\\sum _{i=1} ^{n-1} k_iv_i^T$. \\n\\nOne significant challenge in long-context tasks is for our model to perfectly, accurately retrieve a given piece of information, since the quantity of information we want to remember increases linearly, but our state matrix remains fixed. Suppose we want to retrieve a specific value $v_i$; if our keys are normalized, we can multiply by $k_i^T$ to compute $k_i^T k_i v_i^T = v_i^T$. However, in practice, when we multiply by our matrix $M_{n-1}$, we will also accumulate a residual error given by the second term in\\n$$\\nk_i^TM_{n-1} = k_i^Tk_iv_i^T + \\\\sum _{j \\\\neq i} (k_j^Tk_j)v_i.\\n$$\\nNote that any two randomly selected normalized vectors in $d-$dimensional space has an expected dot product similarity of $1/d$, so retrieval error grows with $O(n/d)$ which becomes prohibitive for large $n$. This also matches empirical findings: gated-convolution linear attention architectures primarily underperform transformers on associative recall tasks ([Arora et. al., 2023](https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.04927)); for example, predicting the next token in “Hakuna Matata means no worries Hakuna Matata it means no \\\\_.\\" ; This means that linear attention will struggle to perform precise tasks across thousands or millions of tokens. \\n\\n# Deriving a Regression\\n\\nUltimately, if we want to address this fatal flaw in linear attention design, then it makes sense to spell out the retrieval error we want to minimize. Assuming that keys are normalized, we demonstrated in the last section that we can retrieve a specific value for a token position by multiplying the state matrix for that key, as $M_{n-1}k_i \\\\approx v_i$. Thus, to minimize retrieval loss, it seems reasonable to minimize $\\\\lVert M_{n-1}k_{n-1} - v_{n-1} \\\\rVert$, which turns out to be what the Delta update rule $M_{n} = M_{n-1} - \\\\beta_nS_{n-1}k_nk_n^T + \\\\beta_nv_nk_n^T$ minimizes ([Yang, 2024](https://sustcsonglin.github.io/blog/2024/deltanet-1/#)). However, it would be nice to extend this more generally to ensure continued recall across all keys and values; the most general form of this, within the L2 framework, would be \\n$$\\n\\\\min \\\\sum _{i=1} ^{n-1} \\\\alpha_i \\\\lVert M_{n-1}k_{i} - v_{i} \\\\rVert\\n$$\\nwhere $(a_i)$ are learnable parameters. \\n\\nBefore we continue further, why do we care so much about key-value retrieval in the first place? We\'ll revisit this assumption later, but we do have evidence that transformers will naturally solve a similar regression through in-context learning anyways. \\n\\nMesaNet fully leverages this optimization \\n\\n### Muon-Optimized Updates\\n\\nThe Muon optimizer ([Bernstein, Newhouse, 2025](https://jeremybernste.in/writing/deriving-muon)) has seen superb acclaim and has seen widespread use in frontier open-source models like [Kimi K2](https://moonshotai.github.io/Kimi-K2/). My intuition for why Muon is effective is that \\n\\nATLAS ([Behrouz, 2025](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.23735)) is an excellent example of \\n\\n# Regression Alternatives\\n\\nNow, let\'s tackle the assumption that the best way to design our architecture is through minimizing the regression we gave above. Note that even though the exponentials in softmax attention *do* cause differences between query-key similarities to be amplified when weighting values, attention layers also serve as sequence mixers which transfer information between tokens. The temperature $\\\\tau$ in attention serves this exact function, as it determines how much mixing vs. exact retrieval we want to do. The fact that $\\\\tau > 0$, which causes $o_n = v_t$ where $t = \\\\text{argmax } q_nk_i$, is almost never the case, indicates that the objective we\'ve designed is not optimal, even though in traditional softmax attention, the solution that minimizes the regression is to set $\\\\tau = 0.$ \\n\\nUnfortunately, we still don\'t have a theoretically grounded answer to what the correct combination of mixing vs. retrieval is, and methods today are highly empirical (namely, involving learnable parameters like temperature). In this section, I\'ve documented some empirical strategies that have been tried to improve long-context reasoning.\\n\\n### Persistent Memory\\n\\nWe return to our first example of vanilla linear attention. One downside, which causes the $O(n/d)$ retrieval error as previously observed, is that we can never delete keys and values that are unneeded, and all added key-value outer products into our state matrix have equal weighting. One way to get around this is through \\n\\n- NAMM (KV cache compression)\\n- HMA\\n### Polynomial Feature Maps\\n\\n- LMUFormer\\n- Fourier transform\\n### Replacing the Matrix\\n\\n#### Test-Time Training\\n\\n#### Caching Keys and Values\\n\\n- MLaS\\n- Memory Mosaics\\n- RETRO\\n### Hybrid Architectures\\n\\n#### Gating\\n- Selective attention (Big-bird, NSA)\\n-  Reformer (efficient transformer) uses LSH\\n\\nWe don’t need to compute all attention scores; we just compute\\n\\n- Global tokens: e.g. (CLS token and question tokens)\\n- All tokens attend to a window of w neighboring tokens\\n- All tokens attend to some set of random tokens\\n\\n# Future Directions\\n\\n### Back to the Regression\\n\\nWe return to our the minimization in our discussion of MesaNet,\\n$$\\n\\\\min \\\\sum _{i=1} ^{n-1} \\\\alpha_i \\\\lVert M_{n-1}k_{i} - v_{i} \\\\rVert.\\n$$\\nInspired by approaches mentioned in the Persistent Memory section (a la [Titans](https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.00663)), it would be interesting to find out if the model can benefit from additional, auxiliary keys and values; the regression could be then rewritten as\\n$$\\n\\\\min \\\\left( \\\\sum _{i=1} ^{n-1} \\\\alpha_i \\\\lVert M_{n-1}k_{i} - v_{i} \\\\rVert + \\\\sum _{i=1} ^N \\\\beta_i \\\\lVert M_{n-1}\\\\hat{k}_{i} - \\\\hat{v}_{i} \\\\rVert \\\\right).\\n$$\\nwhere the $N$ auxiliary keys and values are $(\\\\hat{k_i})$ and $(\\\\hat{v_i})$. We can imagine this being very useful in a variety of scenarios, since this enables context-length extensions as we can compress information from previous context windows into our auxiliary values before we reset $M$, or to pull specific, memorized information learning during training (a la [Memory Layers at Scale](https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.09764)). We already know that in certain situations, solving the direct regression \\n\\nWe might also question the choice of norm. Traditionally, this is preferred due to its compatibility with GPU hardware, since it maps to a dot product $\\\\lVert x\\\\rVert = x^T x$ and thus can exploit fused multiply-add paths that the L1 norm cannot. However, the difference between the L2 and L1 norm is not major, and architectures have been proposed that use the Huber loss ([Behrouz, 2025](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.13173)), which is given by \\n\\n### Utilizing the CPU\\n\\n- RAG\\n- Million dummies\\n- SSD\\n\\n### Data-Architecture Co-design\\n\\nUnfortunately, high-quality long-context data is scarce. To fully make use of the our existing data, we can design hierarchical strategies to merge shorter chunks of data, and use curriculum learning (gradually increasing difficulty over time) as our pre-training schedule. (Note that curriculum learning is also how YaRN is used to increase context lengths).\\n# Conclusion\\n\\nI believe that efficient and performative long-context reasoning is one of the most important problems to solve on the path towards super-intelligence. If we trace back the last decade of AI, we have seen one consistent pattern — The Bitter Lesson, which posits that in the limit, models that effectively scale with increased computation will win. The genius of self-attention is that it lends itself remarkably to parallelization, and was designed with specialized GPU hardware in mind. This enabled us to see actualized performance gains with increased compute and larger model sizes, which were not replicated in RNNs and LSTMs at the time. The Chinchilla scaling laws hammered the point home: despite minor architectural differences, model performance really just boiled down to an equation involving dataset size and compute (which governs parameter count), and past a certain point, compute is much easier to reliably continue to source than high-quality data. The recent innovation in chain-of-thought models is simply an extension of this philosophy: it enables us to continue to scale compute for increased performance gains by using generated tokens as a \\"scratchpad\\", in a way that universal transformers of the past couldn\'t. My opinion is that this trend will continue, and optimized, efficient architectures for ever-longer chain-of-thought traces will be the next crucial innovation for tasks like building fully autonomous ML engineers or researchers.\\n\\nThis is also my first go at technical writing, so I\'d love to discuss any thoughts or feedback, and apologizes for the rough patches :) ","vaultPath":"Towards Long-Context Reasoning.md","public":true,"readingTime":"10 min read"},{"id":"L1VzZXJzL2tldmlud2FuZy9PYnNpZGlhbi9DYW1wIEtlc2VtLm1k","slug":"camp-kesem","section":"misc","sections":[],"title":"Camp Kesem","date":"2025-08-26T16:31:09.840Z","tags":["memory"],"displayUrl":null,"image":"/Images/kesem.jpg?v=1756223976386","description":null,"links":[],"markdown":"---\\n  \\nThis summer, I had the pleasure of being a counselor for the olden teen group for Camp Kesem, a sleepaway camp for kids whose parents have or had cancer. It\'s funny because even though I was supposed to be a role model and emotional supporter for the kids, they more than served that role for me, whether by inviting me into big group hugs, offering a shoulder to lean on during empowerment, participating in my Moon games, laughing at my terribly corny jokes, saving up pies for me, and so much more. Those kids were my heroes, and seeing them battle through so much adversity, whether physical, financial, or emotional, and continue to try their best and support their peers through migraines and crutches and familial turmoil inspires me to try my best to create a space where everyone feels safe to be themselves and find hope. \\n\\nPictured is me eating a taco (??) with mayo, ketchup, chocolate, and five minutes of salt shaking for Chopped, the teen cooking competition :\')","vaultPath":"Camp Kesem.md","public":true,"readingTime":"1 min read"}]}');

